{
  "dom_id": "step-534132",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution",
  "problem": "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{5-x} = -2$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{4-x} = 5$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{2x+1} = -1$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and III state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and III have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
